.TH BLOCKWRITE 1 "October 2026" "hd-backup" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
blockwrite \- write a stream to a tape drive in blocks of 4 MiB
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B blockwrite
.I device
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B blockwrite
reads a stream from its standard input and writes it to
.I device
in blocks of exactly 4 MiB, one
.BR write (2)
per block, with
.BR O_DIRECT .
On tape, every block becomes one record of the drive.
The last block is filled up with zeros.
.PP
.BR hd\-backup (1)
writes its tarballs to tape through
.BR mbuffer (1)
and
.BR blockwrite .
.PP
A short write is an error and is never continued, as the rest of the block
would start on an address that is not aligned.
When the tape runs full, the write fails with
.BR "No space left on device" .
.PP
Closing a tape device writes the filemark, so a failure to close the device
is an error too.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
At the end, also on an error, it prints the bytes it wrote to the device in
full blocks on standard error:
.PP
.RS
Total bytes written: \fIN\fR
.RE
.PP
.B hd-backup
reads this to know how much of the tarball is on the tape when it runs full.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the whole stream was written, 1 on an error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd\-backup (1),
.BR mt\-st (1)
